  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Rate Limits**\n\nI\'m currently encountering a 429 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED error from the Gemini API. It seems the `gemini-2.5-pro` model might be hitting a rate limit on the free tier. I am planning to test with `gemini-2.5-flash` instead to see if that resolves the issue and helps stay within different quotas.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing more robust error management. Specifically, I\'m switching the backend model to `gemini-2.5-flash` to potentially circumvent the 429 quota error and implementing a more graceful message for the frontend to handle the error when it is hit, rather than crashing or displaying an unhelpful error. I\'ve updated `server.ts` to reflect the model change and a default error message.\n\n\n**Improving Error Responses**\n\nI\'m now making the error messages more user-friendly. I\'ve updated the error handling to provide a more specific message about connection problems when the Gemini API fails, and include detail if rate limits are hit. The catch block in the code now generates a more informative response to the client. This will avoid the generic \"failed to fetch\" message and better guide the user.\n\n\n**Refining Error Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on providing better Spanish-language error messages for quota limits. I\'m going to return a 200 status with an informational message rather than a 500. This is because the frontend (specifically `App.tsx`) doesn\'t check the response status. Instead, it directly parses the JSON, and a 500 error would break the frontend. I\'ll include the specific error message, in Spanish, about exceeding the Gemini API quota.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 93446610
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
